The organisation notifies authorities, users, and other stakeholders about vulnerabilities and incidents in time. The coordinated vulnerability disclosure policy and the incident management process set the rules for this.

== How to decide if this control is required

This control is required when the organisation must report incidents or vulnerabilities to authorities, or when the users of its products or services must know about them. External communication about security issues is coordinated centrally.

== How to implement

Prepare and maintain the capability to notify:

* authorities, about reportable incidents and vulnerabilities, within the deadlines of the applicable regulation
* users, about incidents and vulnerabilities that affect them, and about the measures that they can take to reduce the impact
* the persons or organisations that maintain the third-party components of the products, including open source components, about vulnerabilities in their components.

For example, NIS2 (Directive (EU) 2022/2555, Article 23) requires an early warning within 24 hours, an incident notification within 72 hours, and a final report within one month after the incident notification of a significant incident.

Record the notification types, the responsibilities, and the internal deadlines in the coordinated vulnerability disclosure policy.

