NOTE: A security advisory about one product is a document of that product. Write it in the development project of the product.

Check that the notification procedures are defined and communicated, and that people are assigned to the responsibilities. Check that there is a record of each notification, and that all reportable vulnerabilities and incidents have been reported.

{{#if this.craGuidance}}
== EU Cyber Resilience Act

The reporting obligations of the CRA (Regulation (EU) 2024/2847, Article 14) apply from 11 September 2026. The manufacturer of a product with digital elements notifies an actively exploited vulnerability in the product, and a severe incident that has an impact on the security of the product. The notifications go at the same time to the CSIRT designated as coordinator and to ENISA, through the single reporting platform (Article 16).

For an actively exploited vulnerability (Article 14(2)), send:

* an early warning within 24 hours of becoming aware of it
* a vulnerability notification within 72 hours of becoming aware of it
* a final report no later than 14 days after a corrective or mitigating measure is available.

For a severe incident (Article 14(4)), send:

* an early warning within 24 hours of becoming aware of it
* an incident notification within 72 hours of becoming aware of it
* a final report within one month after the incident notification.

After becoming aware of the vulnerability or the incident, inform the impacted users, and where appropriate all users, about it and about the measures that they can take (Article 14(8)).

When the manufacturer finds a vulnerability in a component of the product, including an open source component, it reports the vulnerability to the person or organisation that maintains the component (Article 13(6)).

Write the security advisories of a product with the security advisory template of the EU CRA module, in the development project of the product.
